export { dailyMixedPool, _dcPickDistinct, altLinePool, sgpPool, genParlay, allPropEV, americanFair, fmtAm, unifiedPicks, getMinEdge, setMinEdge, trackerRates, betRates, hitterScore, evPerDollar, impliedProb, breakevenAmerican, fetchedOddsFor, oddsGet, oddsManual, oddsSet, oddsMatchSlate, oddsAgeMin, oddsStale, oddsIsAuto, ladderPick, ladderState, ladderSave, _ladDay, _ladDaysLeft, _decToAm, americanToDecimal, scanStep1_games, scanStep2_arms, scanStep3_contact, scanStep4_power, CV_PROPS, cvBlock, cvMeasured, cvPasses, convergeVal, cvLiftNote, ksProjection, calibratedHRprob, smoothPropProb, pmStarters, pmArsenal, pmDefaultSel, pmAgg, _ptName, _pmScale, hrpDays, hrpAggregate, wilson, hrpWeights, hrpPlayerSignals, hrpLogTallies, splitVisible, splitColumns, splitValue, splitFormat, splitLabels, splitState };
// Guide & FAQ from hr-board, [title, body HTML].
export const GUIDE=[["What is \"heat\"?","Heat is a <b>0–100 score</b> for how likely a hitter is to homer tonight, built from four Statcast signals — pull-in-air rate, exit velocity, barrel rate, and ideal launch angle — blended over his last two weeks. <b>70+</b> is elite, <b>55–69</b> solid, under 40 is a fade. It's the backbone of the app and it's <b>frozen</b> — every other feature is a lens on top of it, never a change to it."],["Badges & the ELITE tag","Badges flag why a hitter stands out: <b>HOT / COOL</b> (form rising or cooling), <b>DUE</b> (hitting it harder than results show), <b>LOCK</b> (elite earned contact), <b>PLATOON</b> (handedness edge on tonight's pitcher), <b>HR vs SP / PEN</b> (has taken this staff deep), and <b>CROSSHAIR</b> (zone overlap with the pitcher's weak spots). Each badge also shows its <b>measured lift</b> — how much more often it's actually led to a HR in the backtest."],["The spray field on player cards","Each card draws <b>tonight's ballpark</b> — its real outfield fence — and plots the hitter's last two weeks of batted balls by angle and distance. <b>Gold dots are home runs</b>, orange dots are balls that <b>would clear tonight's fence</b> where they were hit, gray are other air balls. It shows at a glance whether his power plays in this specific park."],["Reading the data (deep dive)","Every card shows core signals with <b>MLB percentile chips</b> (P88 = top 12%) and 2-week / season / career splits, a <b>zone-damage grid</b> (xwOBA by pitch location), plate-discipline percentiles, and the full <b>opposing-arm vulnerability table</b> — barrel, EV, HR/PA and whiff rates allowed, velo trend, and platoon splits. If you want to verify the model's read yourself, it's all there."],["Qualified Bets & edge","These are spots where the <b>book's price beats our fair price</b> — the only real edge in betting. Each is sized by <b>half-Kelly</b> off your bankroll, and HR bets show <b>line movement</b> (steam toward you, or drifting away). If you just want the model's best value plays, this is the list."],["Top Picks vs Qualified Bets","<b>Top Picks</b> ranks every prop by how <b>likely</b> it is to hit — pick from the top when you just want winners. <b>Qualified Bets</b> ranks by <b>value</b> — where the price is wrong. The sweet spot is a play high on both. Likelihood wins more often; value makes money long-term."],["Parlay Builder & Long Ball Jackpot","<b>Parlay Builder</b> generates a same-game or cross-game parlay from your chosen prop mix, leg count, and goal (most likely, best value, or biggest payout). <b>Long Ball Jackpot</b> targets the \"longest HR of the day\" contest — it finds the highest distance ceilings and flags the under-the-radar ones that pay the most if they win."],["Game Lines (moneyline)","A run model projects each game from lineups, starter and bullpen quality, park, weather, and handedness platoon, giving a win probability, total, and first-5 line. The moneyline is the <b>sharpest market in baseball</b>, so treat it as informational — the calibration backtest in Results is the honest verdict on its accuracy."],["How accurate is it, really?","Everything is backtested and tracked. The <b>Tracker</b> shows whether hitters in each heat band homer at the predicted rate; the <b>backtests</b> show HR, prop, and moneyline accuracy across the season. The misses are shown too — an honest tool is the point."],["Signal Convergence — what it is and why it works","The single best-validated tool in the app. It counts how many <b>independent</b> reads point at the same hitter tonight, grouped into <b>families</b> so correlated evidence can't be counted twice. Across 28,000 graded player-games the backtest is clean and monotonic: <b>0 families 0.75x · 1 family 1.06x · 2 families 1.33x · 3 families 1.56x · 4 families 2.01x</b> the base HR rate. The families are Contact quality, Launch profile, Form, Zone matchup, Arsenal matchup, Fits this park, Top of the order, Opposing arm and Environment."],["COV badges — the pink and green numbers","Your convergence rank for tonight. <b class=\"cov2 cov2-heat\" style=\"padding:1px 5px\">COV#</b> in <b>pink</b> is the rank <b>with heat</b> — all evidence combined. <b class=\"cov2 cov2-noheat\" style=\"padding:1px 5px\">COV#</b> in <b>neon green</b> is the rank <b>without heat</b> — only evidence the core model isn't already using. COV1 green is the hitter tonight with the most non-heat evidence behind him. When a player is high on both, everything agrees; when the green rank is far better than the pink one, the market usually hasn't caught up, which is where the prices live."],["'With heat' vs 'Without heat'","Heat correlates with almost every other read, so it can drown them out. Excluding it asks a sharper question: does the <b>rest</b> of the evidence agree on its own? The backtest says yes — with heat removed, 1 measured signal still grades <b>1.44x</b> and 2+ grades <b>1.77x</b>. That's the proof the other signals carry real, independent information."],["'Most evidence' vs 'Overlooked'","<b>Most evidence</b> ranks by raw agreement — correct if you want the highest HR probability, but it leans toward established everyday hitters, because those are the players with complete evidence profiles. <b>Overlooked</b> ranks by evidence the core model <b>hasn't</b> priced in: hitters heat already loves get pushed down. On a typical slate that swaps roughly two-thirds of the top 20 and drops median heat from 52 to 30. Lower raw probability, better prices — that's the trade."],["Measured vs Provisional signals","The bold number counts only <b>measured</b> signals — ones your own backtest has graded (heat bands, POWER at 1.75x, HOT/LOCK at 1.36x). The small <b>+n</b> counts <b>provisional</b> families that look sensible but haven't been graded yet. They're kept separate on purpose so nothing looks stronger than the evidence supports. Two badges, <b>DUE</b> and <b>MAY COOL</b>, are excluded from counting entirely — measured at 1.01x and 1.00x over 11,000+ player-games, they're noise."],["Launch profile — why it's separate from contact","How hard a hitter hits the ball and whether he <b>lifts it to the pull side</b> are nearly unrelated: pull-air rate correlates just <b>-0.11</b> with exit velo and ideal launch angle <b>+0.01</b>. (Hard-hit% is +0.81 with EV, which is why that one sits inside Contact.) So launch geometry — pull-air, ideal angle, pull-barrel, fly-ball profile — gets its own family. The guy who scorches everything into the ground and the guy who lifts it are different bets."],["Punish score — green-on-green","On the player card: does this hitter mash the pitches this arm both <b>throws most</b> and <b>gets hurt on</b>? Each pitch is weighted by usage x vulnerability (SLG, barrel% and HR rate allowed), then scored by what the hitter actually does to that pitch. 50 is neutral. A low score doesn't mean a bad hitter — it means his damage doesn't land where <b>this particular arm</b> is exposed, which can flip completely tomorrow."],["'Built to punish this arsenal' & near misses","The hitter's full line against only the pitches tonight's starter actually throws his side: home runs, SLG, ISO, hard-hit%, barrel% and <b>near misses</b>. A near miss is a ball hit <b>350+ feet that didn't leave</b> — warning-track contact that clears in a lot of parks. It's valuable because it says the swing was there and the result was park or wind, which is exactly what changes on a different night."],["Zone matchup — the 13-cell map","The strike zone's nine cells <b>plus the four chase quadrants</b> outside it (Z11-Z14), which are roughly half of all pitches thrown. Your damage map is laid over where the arm actually lives, split by your handedness, with a stat selector (Power Score, HR rate, xwOBAcon, SLG, ISO, barrel%, hard-hit%, EV, distance). <b>Matchup edges</b> tells you how many of his most-used zones are ones you punish."],["Team vs Pitch Mix","The starter's arsenal split by batter handedness — most arms attack lefties and righties completely differently — with every hitter in the opposing lineup graded against those exact pitches. Tap pitches to filter. There's also a <b>pitch usage history</b> chart: an arm who has quietly doubled his slider usage is a different pitcher than his season line claims."],["Batted-ball log","Every recent batted ball on the player card: date, pitcher, arm, pitch type, exit velo, launch angle, distance, bat speed, pitch velo, result, trajectory, and <b>how many of the 30 MLB parks</b> that ball would have cleared."],["Ladder Challenge","Roll a small stake toward a target by a deadline, one bet a day, winnings rolled over. It computes the American odds you need each day, finds the <b>safest</b> leg or combo that clears it across HR/hits/HRR/Ks/moneyline, and lets you <b>edit each leg's odds</b> to match your book. A miss resets to your starting stake. It also shows your realistic chance of running the whole ladder — usually small, and it says so."],["Hit props — what the model actually looks for","The hit profile is close to the <b>opposite</b> of the home run profile, and the model is built that way. Power wants pulled, lifted contact; hits want flat contact sprayed across the whole field. It weights <b>xBA</b> heaviest, then <b>opposite-field rate</b> (25%+ is above MLB average and beats defensive positioning), <b>spray balance</b>, and the <b>BABIP launch window</b> of −4° to 26° — deliberately flatter than the 23–34° power band, so the hit model doesn't inherit the HR model's preference for lift. It also credits <b>sprint speed</b> (29+ ft/s turns topped grounders into infield singles) and the opposing arm's <b>strikeout and walk rates</b>, because a hitter can't single if the at-bat ends without a ball in play. Honest caveat: hit props graded weakest of all the models in the backtest, topping out near 1.11x, so treat them as the thinnest edge in the app."],["Strikeout props — read this before betting them","These were recalibrated after the backtest showed the projections ran hot. Even a top-tier arm averages about <b>6.1</b> strikeouts and clears O6.5 only <b>44%</b> of the time. Probabilities now come from measured backtest outcomes rather than a Poisson guess, and the suggested line always sits <b>below</b> the projection — a previous bug could suggest a line the model itself expected to miss."],["Team defense & the run model","The moneyline model includes handedness platoon matchups and an <b>OAA-style team defense</b> read (how many hits each defense saves versus expected). It beats a naive baseline on Brier score with tight calibration. The <b>total</b> does not — its error is about a full run worse than the market, so treat game totals as informational only."],["Is the ball changing?","In the HR Calendar. Average HR distance barely moves even when the ball genuinely changes, because a deader ball means only the best-struck balls still clear — the pool re-selects itself. So this tracks <b>carry</b> instead: how far every well-struck ball (95+ mph, 20-35 degrees) actually travels versus what that contact should produce. If carry moves while exit velo stays flat, it's the ball. A slow seasonal drift is mostly weather; a sharp step-change is the real signal."],["Bankroll & responsible betting","Set your bankroll in settings and the app sizes every bet with <b>half-Kelly</b> — never more than the math says. This is an analytics tool, not a guarantee; no model wins every night. Bet within your means, and only what you can afford to lose."]];
